item.content:encoded:
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Building Blastradius for Graph Hacks with FalkorDB&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fp5uw94amq6y5j6m06z7c.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fp5uw94amq6y5j6m06z7c.png" alt="Blastradius tracing a critical advisory through the dependency graph to an internet-facing service" width="800" height="450"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Live demo:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://13-203-50-195.sslip.io" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://13-203-50-195.sslip.io&lt;/a&gt; · &lt;strong&gt;Code:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://github.com/NishaadG/blastradius" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://github.com/NishaadG/blastradius&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every time a new CVE lands, someone asks the same four questions: &lt;strong&gt;Are we affected? Through which dependency? Who owns that service? Did we already deal with something like this?&lt;/strong&gt; Most tools answer with a list. The honest answer is a &lt;em&gt;path&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The problem with "similar text"
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Most agent demos retrieve context by embedding similarity. That is fine for "find the doc that sounds like this". It is the wrong tool for &lt;em&gt;"does this vulnerable package reach a service that faces the internet?"&lt;/em&gt;, because that is a question about &lt;strong&gt;connections&lt;/strong&gt;, and similarity can't prove a connection exists or doesn't.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A graph can. In Blastradius the question is one traversal:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight cypher"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;MATCH&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="ss"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="py"&gt;a:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Advisory&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="ss"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="py"&gt;id:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;$id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="ss"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="ss"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="py"&gt;e:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;AFFECTS&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="ss"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="ss"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="py"&gt;p:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Package&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="ss"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;MATCH&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="ss"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="py"&gt;s:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Service&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="ss"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;WITH&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="ss"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;e&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="ss"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="ss"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;s&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="ss"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;shortestPath&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="ss"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;s&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="ss"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="ss"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;:DEPENDS_ON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="m"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;..&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="m"&gt;15&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="ss"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="ss"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="ss"&gt;))&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;AS&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;WHERE&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;IS&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="ow"&gt;NOT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;NULL&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;RETURN&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;s.name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="ss"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;p.name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="ss"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;p.version&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="ss"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;e.fixed&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="ss"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;length&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="ss"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="ss"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="ss"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;n&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="ow"&gt;IN&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;nodes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="ss"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="ss"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;coalesce&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="ss"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;n.key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="ss"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;n.name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="ss"&gt;)]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Run it on a real project (Google's Online Boutique, with real lockfiles and real OSV advisories) and a critical &lt;code&gt;protobufjs&lt;/code&gt; RCE resolves, in tens of milliseconds, to:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;frontend → checkoutservice → paymentservice → @opentelemetry/exporter-otlp-grpc → @grpc/proto-loader → protobufjs@6.11.4&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;with the internet-facing entry point, the owning team, and the fixed version, all from the same graph.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Proof by absence
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Ask about Log4Shell and the same query returns &lt;strong&gt;zero rows&lt;/strong&gt;. That is not a failure; it is the answer. Nothing in the graph depends on a Maven package, so the verdict is &lt;code&gt;NOT_AFFECTED&lt;/code&gt;, with the empty traversal on screen as the evidence. Ask about an advisory for a package you &lt;em&gt;do&lt;/em&gt; run, but at a patched version, and the graph explains &lt;em&gt;why&lt;/em&gt; it's safe: the package is present, the version is outside the affected range.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The LLM doesn't get to decide
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The verdict (&lt;code&gt;AFFECTED / NOT_AFFECTED / NEEDS_HUMAN&lt;/code&gt;) comes from plain rules over graph evidence. The LLM, optional and free-tier, only chooses which tool to call and phrases the result. It never sees a database credential or writes Cypher; the read path is rejected for writes and enforced read-only by the database too. Remediation actions are proposals that wait for a human.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Memory that is also a graph
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When a human approves a fix, the decision is written back as a &lt;code&gt;Case&lt;/code&gt; node linked to the advisory, the affected services and the playbook used. Days later a &lt;em&gt;different&lt;/em&gt; &lt;code&gt;protobufjs&lt;/code&gt; advisory arrives, and a three-hop traversal &lt;code&gt;(Case)-[:ABOUT]-&amp;gt;(Advisory)-[:AFFECTS]-&amp;gt;(Package)&lt;/code&gt; finds the earlier case. The agent cites it and reuses the playbook; the playbook's success counter ticks up. Memory, dependency data and ownership live in one graph, so the agents' handoffs (&lt;code&gt;Step-[:HANDS_OFF_TO]-&amp;gt;Step&lt;/code&gt;) are queryable too.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Each tenant gets its &lt;strong&gt;own FalkorDB graph&lt;/strong&gt;, so isolation is structural rather than a &lt;code&gt;WHERE tenant = …&lt;/code&gt; you hope nobody forgets.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


A company brain with receipts
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Ownership comes from &lt;code&gt;CODEOWNERS&lt;/code&gt;, expertise from commit history, and dependency decisions link to the exact commit that made them. A &lt;code&gt;SUPERSEDES&lt;/code&gt; edge marks decisions that were later overtaken, so the answer to "why do we pin this?" says whether the reasoning is still current.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Commits say what changed, not why, so a build step also reads the pull request behind each dependency change and has Gemini extract the decision and the stated reason. The model is boxed in: it may only restate what the PR says, an empty rationale stays empty, and a package is linked to a decision only if its name literally appears in the PR or commit text. Two evals enforce this, and the second one caught the model inventing a subject (&lt;code&gt;otlp-exporters&lt;/code&gt;, a paraphrase, not a package) on the first run. Now "why did we upgrade golang.org/x/net?" is answered with the CVEs and the PR links.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The topology is discovered, not typed in
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Early on I hand-wrote which service calls which. That's the weakest part of any demo, so I replaced it: Blastradius now reads the project's own deployment files. In Online Boutique's Kubernetes manifests, &lt;code&gt;PRODUCT_CATALOG_SERVICE_ADDR=productcatalogservice:3550&lt;/code&gt; is a call and a &lt;code&gt;LoadBalancer&lt;/code&gt; Service is an internet exposure. In the OpenTelemetry demo's Compose file, bare env names resolve through &lt;code&gt;.env&lt;/code&gt;, and only a real &lt;code&gt;host:container&lt;/code&gt; port mapping counts as exposure. Checked against my old hand-written list, discovery recovered 100% of the OpenTelemetry edges (and found 7 the docs never mention), 100% of Bank of Anthos's (whose addresses hide inside ConfigMaps and &lt;code&gt;jdbc:&lt;/code&gt; URLs) and 94% of Boutique's; the one it misses is in no deployment file at all, and the eval report says so.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Any public repo, including the paths manifests hide
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Paste &lt;code&gt;owner/name&lt;/code&gt; and Blastradius finds the manifests, asks &lt;a href="https://deps.dev" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;deps.dev&lt;/a&gt; for the resolved dependency tree when a manifest lists only direct dependencies, and builds a tenant graph. Pointed at a deliberately vulnerable Java app, it finds Log4Shell as &lt;code&gt;app → spring-boot-starter-log4j2 → log4j-core@2.14.1&lt;/code&gt;. The build file never mentions log4j-core; a static parser sees nothing. A graph of resolved dependencies sees the whole chain.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


One graph, many AI clients
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The same toolkit is exposed as an MCP server, so Claude Desktop, Claude Code or any MCP client can ask the graph directly, and as a tool-calling loop for the chat box. The model can only call validated, read-only tools; it never sees Cypher.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How do we know it's right?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Ground truth is computed &lt;em&gt;without&lt;/em&gt; the graph from the raw lockfiles, cached OSV data, the discovered topology and &lt;code&gt;CODEOWNERS&lt;/code&gt;, then compared to the product: affected-service sets and verdicts for every advisory in three real projects (the OpenTelemetry demo, Online Boutique and Bank of Anthos), that every returned path is a real chain of lockfile edges, exposure distances, ownership, transitive callers, memory reuse, and tenant and session isolation. 57 of 57 pass, plus 42 unit tests and guardrail checks (write queries are rejected on the agent path).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Under load, honestly
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Track 2 mentions high-concurrency shared state, so I benchmarked it: 10, 50 and 100 agents hammering one graph. Reads stay fast; FalkorDB serialises writes, so write latency queues. At 100 agents my first run saw about 2% of operations rejected with &lt;code&gt;Max pending queries exceeded&lt;/code&gt;. That's FalkorDB protecting itself, so the client now retries with jittered backoff and counts every retry in the results instead of hiding them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The "is the graph decoration?" test
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Stop the FalkorDB container. The UI turns red and nothing answers: no cache, no fallback, by design. Start it, and everything resumes. If removing the database leaves the product working, the database was decoration. Here, it is the product.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Reachable, not just present
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For Go services, Blastradius runs Google's &lt;code&gt;govulncheck&lt;/code&gt; and stores the call-graph result on the edge between service and advisory. A P0 whose vulnerable function is never called is demoted with the reason; one that is called shows the actual stack, from &lt;code&gt;main&lt;/code&gt; down to the vulnerable function.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


It notices, and it blocks
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A watcher re-queries OSV on a schedule and flags advisories published since the graph was built. A CLI and GitHub Action analyse a checkout, write SARIF and fail a pull request only for findings that change &lt;em&gt;introduces&lt;/em&gt; compared to the base branch.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


SBOM in, SBOM out
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Not every team can point a tool at its source. So Blastradius also takes a CycloneDX or SPDX file: each component's purl becomes a &lt;code&gt;Package&lt;/code&gt; node, the SBOM's dependency list becomes &lt;code&gt;DEPENDS_ON&lt;/code&gt; edges, and from there it is the same graph and the same traversals. It goes the other way too: any tenant exports as CycloneDX 1.5 with the advisories that apply. The eval exports each of the three projects, re-imports the file, and checks that the packages and edges come back identical.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


A free-tier model, shared with strangers
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The hosted demo runs on Gemini's free tier, and every visitor shares one quota. Instead of letting a busy minute turn into errors, the server budgets model calls across everyone and backs off for a short while after a failure. When the model is out, the same tools answer through a deterministic router, and the UI says which one answered and why. Because the verdicts never depended on the model, the answer to "are we affected?" is the same either way.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What's next
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Function-level reachability beyond Go, and building the &lt;code&gt;CALLS&lt;/code&gt; edges from OpenTelemetry traces instead of reading them from deployment files.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Try it
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Open the &lt;a href="https://13-203-50-195.sslip.io" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;live demo&lt;/a&gt; and take the guided tour, or paste a public &lt;code&gt;owner/name&lt;/code&gt; repository. To run it yourself, it is &lt;code&gt;docker compose up&lt;/code&gt; plus one build command, and it works offline from a committed snapshot. The README has the data model, every Cypher query the product depends on, and the eval report.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Built with FalkorDB, Cypher, FastAPI, Next.js and Cytoscape. Everything runs locally for $0. Developed with Claude Code as a coding assistant (disclosed per the hackathon rules).&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What I Built
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Meet &lt;strong&gt;AquaDrop&lt;/strong&gt; — a hydration reminder app designed to make drinking water regularly easier, simpler, and more consistent.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We often get busy with college, work, coding, gaming, or everyday tasks and forget something as basic as drinking enough water. AquaDrop aims to solve this problem by making hydration reminders accessible through a clean, friendly interface.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Instead of overwhelming users with complicated health dashboards, AquaDrop focuses on helping them build a small but meaningful daily habit.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Key features
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;💧 &lt;strong&gt;Hydration reminders:&lt;/strong&gt; Get reminders to take a water break throughout the day.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;⏰ &lt;strong&gt;Reminder controls:&lt;/strong&gt; Manage your reminders and snooze them when you need a little more time.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;🎨 &lt;strong&gt;Light, dark, and system themes:&lt;/strong&gt; Choose an appearance that suits your preferences.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;🔔 &lt;strong&gt;Notification and sound experience:&lt;/strong&gt; Make reminders more noticeable.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;✨ &lt;strong&gt;A friendly interface:&lt;/strong&gt; A blue-and-white visual identity with a water-drop-inspired design.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;🌱 &lt;strong&gt;Room to grow:&lt;/strong&gt; AquaDrop can evolve into a more comprehensive hydration companion with intake tracking, progress analytics, and personalized coaching.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Who is AquaDrop for?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Students, developers, remote workers, gamers, and anyone who spends long hours concentrating on a screen.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


The side-hustle opportunity
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AquaDrop could grow into a freemium product: essential hydration reminders for free, with optional premium features such as advanced scheduling, detailed progress insights, and personalization.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The goal is to keep the basic habit-building experience accessible while giving users a reason to upgrade if they find additional features valuable.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Demo
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;💧 &lt;strong&gt;Try AquaDrop live:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aqua-drop-seven.vercel.app/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://aqua-drop-seven.vercel.app/&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Explore the application and experience its hydration-focused interface.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Code
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;💻 &lt;strong&gt;GitHub repository:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://github.com/shivammraj/AquaDrop" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://github.com/shivammraj/AquaDrop&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AquaDrop is an ongoing project, and I want to continue improving it through open-source development, user feedback, and practical experimentation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How I Built It
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I approached AquaDrop as a real product rather than just a landing page or a design exercise.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The focus is on making the core experience straightforward: users should be able to access their reminders, adjust their preferences, and interact with the app without unnecessary complexity.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The project currently has a deployed web experience, and my next priorities include improving reminder reliability, making theme preferences consistent across screens, and expanding hydration tracking.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Where open-source AI fits in
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My longer-term vision is to introduce an AI hydration coach that can help users build more consistent habits through contextual tips and personalized insights.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I want to explore open-weight models such as Gemma and local inference options where appropriate. This could make it possible to experiment with AI-powered assistance while retaining greater control over model deployment, costs, and data handling.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Transparency matters:&lt;/strong&gt; I am distinguishing the current application's functionality from planned AI features. I won't claim that an open-weight model is already integrated unless that integration is implemented and tested.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why Does Open Innovation Matter?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Open innovation makes it easier for individual developers to turn ideas into real products.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a side project like AquaDrop, open-source tools and open-weight AI models can help reduce experimentation costs, encourage community contributions, and make it easier to understand and customize the technology behind a product.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;They also create opportunities to experiment with local inference and privacy-conscious approaches instead of depending entirely on a closed AI API.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For me, the important part is not simply adding AI because it is popular. It is finding places where open technology can make AquaDrop more useful, affordable, and accessible.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I want to build something that people can actually use, learn from their feedback, and improve it over time.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What's Next?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My roadmap for AquaDrop includes:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Better reminders:&lt;/strong&gt; Make scheduling, snoozing, and notification behavior more reliable.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Hydration tracking:&lt;/strong&gt; Let users record their water intake and monitor daily progress.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Progress analytics:&lt;/strong&gt; Introduce daily and weekly summaries.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;AI hydration coach:&lt;/strong&gt; Explore an open-weight model for contextual habit-building guidance.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Freemium features:&lt;/strong&gt; Evaluate which advanced features users would genuinely pay for.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Community contributions:&lt;/strong&gt; Make it easier for other developers to help improve the project.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These are planned improvements, not claims that every feature is already available.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Final Thoughts
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AquaDrop started with a simple idea: help people remember to take care of themselves while they work toward their goals.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;As a developer, I spend a lot of time thinking about code, projects, and building things. I wanted to create something that addresses a small everyday problem and has the potential to grow into a useful product.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Hacktoberfest's Side Hustle challenge is an opportunity to take that idea further — from a working application toward a sustainable, user-focused product.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Build useful things. Keep learning. Stay hydrated. 💧&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you try AquaDrop, I'd love to hear what you think and what you'd improve!&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This is a submission for the &lt;a href="https://dev.to/challenges/hacktoberfest-week2-2026-10-12"&gt;Hacktoberfest Open-Source AI Challenge Week 2: Side Hustle&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What I Built
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Solo sellers, makers, and side-hustlers — selling on Etsy, eBay, Shopify, or local markets — spend countless tedious hours writing product titles, descriptions, keyword tags, and SEO metadata. &lt;br&gt;
For someone testing a side hustle or making an extra $200–$500 a month, paying a &lt;strong&gt;$20–$40/month subscription&lt;/strong&gt; for a proprietary AI listing SaaS is a massive friction point that directly eats their margins.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Seller AI&lt;/strong&gt; is an open-source, local-first AI workspace built specifically for small online sellers. It turns raw, bullet-point product notes and photos into marketplace-ready, high-converting product listings in seconds:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;4-in-1 Marketplace Formats&lt;/strong&gt;: Generates listings tailored to the strict rules and character limits of &lt;strong&gt;Generic e-commerce, Shopify, Etsy, and eBay&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Alternative Titles &amp;amp; SEO Metadata&lt;/strong&gt;: Generates multiple catchy title hooks, meta descriptions, and algorithmic search tags.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Multilingual &amp;amp; Regional Commerce&lt;/strong&gt;: Supports English, pure Hindi (Devanagari script), and bilingual listings for sellers targeting regional and global buyers.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Local Vision Analysis&lt;/strong&gt;: Capability-aware image analysis for product photos running on open-weight vision models (like &lt;code&gt;qwen2.5vl:3b&lt;/code&gt;) without sending unreleased product photos to third-party clouds.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Zero Lock-In &amp;amp; Complete Privacy&lt;/strong&gt;: Client data and product ideas never leave the machine. Listings are stored locally in the browser (&lt;code&gt;localStorage&lt;/code&gt;), with 1-click copy for individual fields, copy-all, and export to &lt;strong&gt;TXT, CSV, and JSON&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Demo
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;deplyed link: &lt;a href="https://seller-ai-ldyl.onrender.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://seller-ai-ldyl.onrender.com&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Code
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;GitHub Repository&lt;/strong&gt;: &lt;a href="https://github.com/Nareshkumawat-star/seller-ai-" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://github.com/Nareshkumawat-star/seller-ai-&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How I Built It
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Open-Source AI Stack
Seller AI is powered entirely by open-weight models running on local inference through Ollama:&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Text &amp;amp; Copywriting: qwen2.5:3b (or llama3.2:3b / mistral:7b) for lightweight, high-efficiency generation.&lt;br&gt;
Vision Extraction: qwen2.5vl:3b for extracting physical attributes, materials, and colors from product photos.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Architecture &amp;amp; Reliability Patterns
Prompt Isolation &amp;amp; Security: User input is strictly isolated inside  blocks with system-level instruction guarding against prompt injection.
Structured JSON Mode: The model is prompted with format: "json". Responses pass through a robust extraction pipeline that handles markdown code fences, comments, and chatter, followed by Zod runtime schema validation.
Language Verification Safeguard: Early tests revealed that smaller models sometimes claim language: "hi" while still returning English body text. We implemented a Unicode script analyzer (lib/ai/language-check.ts) that verifies Devanagari script density, automatically retries if English is detected, and flags unverified outputs.
Hardware-Honest Health Check: /api/health queries Ollama's /api/tags and /api/show to verify if the configured models are actually pulled and vision-capable before the user triggers generation&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why Does Open Innovation Matter?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Zero Marginal Cost for Side Hustlers: When starting an Etsy shop or small handmade store, monthly API or SaaS subscriptions add financial anxiety before making the first sale. Open-weight models running locally cost $0 in recurring subscription fees.&lt;br&gt;
Absolute Privacy for Unreleased Products: Artisans and inventors often draft listings weeks before launching novel designs, handcrafted jewellery, or secret recipes. With local open-source inference, proprietary photos and product concepts never leave their hard drive.&lt;br&gt;
Freedom from Arbitrary Vendor Lock-in: Closed AI APIs frequently change pricing tiers, throttle requests, or deprecate models without warning. With open weights, the seller owns their entire software stack permanently.&lt;br&gt;
Niche Customization: Because marketplace template prompts (lib/config/marketplaces.ts) are open code in the repository, any seller can add specialized rules for their niche platform (e.g., Poshmark, Mercari, Depop) without waiting for a SaaS company to build it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


My Agent Session
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Prize Categories
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;eller AI includes a production-ready render.yaml blueprint configured to deploy the Next.js web application frontend on Render. It enables sellers or teams to deploy their studio front end to the web while pointing to their private, secure remote inference server via the OLLAMA_BASE_URL environment variable.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


Team leader (username):nareshkumawatstar
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The code lives in a public repo and is tested in CI.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Part 4 of the series about my free, MIT-licensed &lt;a href="https://github.com/Fmaes2/dotnet-minimal-api-starter" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;.NET 10 Minimal API starter&lt;/a&gt;. Previous parts: &lt;a href="https://dev.to/fmaes/a-net-10-minimal-api-starter-so-you-can-skip-the-boilerplate-841"&gt;the base template&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="https://dev.to/fmaes/per-user-ownership-in-a-net-minimal-api-and-how-the-user-reaches-your-endpoint-2an1"&gt;per-user ownership&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://dev.to/fmaes/rate-limiting-login-and-registration-in-a-net-10-minimal-api-per-ip-no-extra-packages-32m9"&gt;rate limiting&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The starter had a &lt;code&gt;/health&lt;/code&gt; endpoint that always answered &lt;code&gt;ok&lt;/code&gt;. It told you the process was running, but not whether it could actually serve requests. This post splits it into two endpoints.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Liveness vs readiness
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;/health/live&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;: "is the process alive?" It runs no checks. If the app answers, it is alive. An orchestrator uses this to decide whether to &lt;strong&gt;restart&lt;/strong&gt; the container.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;/health/ready&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;: "can it handle traffic?" It also checks the database. An orchestrator or load balancer uses this to decide whether to &lt;strong&gt;send traffic&lt;/strong&gt; to the instance.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Keeping them separate matters. If the database goes down and your liveness probe also checks it, the orchestrator will restart a perfectly healthy API in a loop and make things worse.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


1. Register the checks
&lt;/h2&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight csharp"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;builder&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Services&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;AddHealthChecks&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;AddDbContextCheck&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;AppDbContext&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"database"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;tags&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"ready"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;AddHealthChecks()&lt;/code&gt; turns on the health check system.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;AddDbContextCheck&amp;lt;AppDbContext&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; tries to connect through your &lt;code&gt;DbContext&lt;/code&gt; and reports &lt;code&gt;Unhealthy&lt;/code&gt; if it can't.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The &lt;code&gt;ready&lt;/code&gt; tag lets us pick this check only for the readiness endpoint.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;AddDbContextCheck&lt;/code&gt; is not in the framework. It comes from &lt;code&gt;Microsoft.Extensions.Diagnostics.HealthChecks.EntityFrameworkCore&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;dotnet add StarterKit.Api package Microsoft.Extensions.Diagnostics.HealthChecks.EntityFrameworkCore
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


2. Map the two endpoints
&lt;/h2&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight csharp"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;app&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;MapHealthChecks&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"/health/live"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;HealthCheckOptions&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;Predicate&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;_&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// run no checks: if the app answers, it is alive&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;app&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;MapHealthChecks&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"/health/ready"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;HealthCheckOptions&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;Predicate&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;check&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;check&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Tags&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;Contains&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"ready"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;Predicate&lt;/code&gt; decides which checks each endpoint runs. They answer &lt;code&gt;200 Healthy&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;503 Unhealthy&lt;/code&gt;, which is what Docker, Kubernetes and load balancers understand.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


3. Testing it, including the failure case
&lt;/h2&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight csharp"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Fact&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;public&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Task&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;Ready_returns_503_when_a_check_fails&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kt"&gt;var&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;client&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;factory&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;WithWebHostBuilder&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;builder&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;builder&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;ConfigureServices&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;services&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;services&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;AddHealthChecks&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;().&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;AddCheck&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s"&gt;"always-failing"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;HealthCheckResult&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;Unhealthy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;tags&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"ready"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;])))&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;CreateClient&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kt"&gt;var&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;client&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;GetAsync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"/health/ready"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;Assert&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;Equal&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;HttpStatusCode&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ServiceUnavailable&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;StatusCode&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Instead of shutting down a database in a test, I register an extra check that always fails and tagged &lt;code&gt;ready&lt;/code&gt;. The test confirms a failing check turns into a 503. Two more tests check that &lt;code&gt;live&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;ready&lt;/code&gt; return 200 against in-memory SQLite.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Gotchas I hit
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Two almost identical package names.&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;code&gt;Microsoft.Extensions.Diagnostics.HealthChecks&lt;/code&gt; is already part of ASP.NET Core. The one you need for &lt;code&gt;AddDbContextCheck&lt;/code&gt; is the &lt;code&gt;...HealthChecks.EntityFrameworkCore&lt;/code&gt; one. I installed the wrong one first.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Version downgrade error (NU1605).&lt;/strong&gt; The new package pulled EF Core 10.0.13 while my project referenced 10.0.12. NuGet refuses to downgrade, so the build failed. The fix is to keep all EF Core and ASP.NET Core packages on the same version.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Test projects don't get implicit usings.&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;code&gt;AddHealthChecks&lt;/code&gt; needs &lt;code&gt;using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;&lt;/code&gt; in the test project, even though the API project doesn't need it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Limitations
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The database check opens a connection on every probe, so don't set the probe interval too aggressively.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The endpoints are public and only return &lt;code&gt;Healthy&lt;/code&gt; / &lt;code&gt;Unhealthy&lt;/code&gt;, without details. Don't expose detailed responses without authentication.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;It only checks the database. If you add Redis, queues or external APIs, each one deserves its own check.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Try it
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Next up: refresh tokens. If you'd rather see something else first, open a Discussion.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is a submission for the Hacktoberfest Open-Source AI Challenge Week 2: Side Hustle&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What I Built&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A customer sends a message:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;“Hi! I need two beige wool carpets, size 6×9 feet. Can you share a quotation?”&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a small business owner, answering that question involves several steps: understanding the requirements, checking the catalogue, finding prices, calculating totals, and preparing a document.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I built QuoteCraft to bring those steps into one workspace.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;QuoteCraft helps small sellers turn customer enquiries into editable, professional quotations. The first version uses a carpet business as its example, but the workflow could also serve furniture shops, printers, and other businesses with product catalogues.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Users can:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Manage products, specifications, and prices.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Store customer details.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Paste an enquiry and request a quotation draft.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Review matched products and edit quantities or rates.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Add custom line items.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Configure tax, payment terms, and delivery estimates.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Save quotations and track their status.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Export a quotation through the browser’s Save as PDF option.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Who would pay for it? A small business owner who prepares similar quotations repeatedly and wants to reduce manual entry while keeping control over the final price.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My initial business hypothesis is a paid setup and customization service, with ongoing support as an option. I haven’t validated willingness to pay yet.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Demo(frontend)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Demo link or video: &lt;a href="https://quotecraft-rose-nine.vercel.app/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://quotecraft-rose-nine.vercel.app/&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A sample walkthrough:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Open the product catalogue.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Paste a customer enquiry.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Generate a draft.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Review the products, quantities, and prices.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Save the quotation and export it as a PDF.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The app includes two distinct drafting modes:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Local AI mode: calls an open-weight model through Ollama.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Demo mode: uses simple keyword matching to test the application without a model.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Demo mode is not AI inference. A deployed frontend and backend still need access to an Ollama service for AI mode to work.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Code&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The project repository: &lt;a href="https://github.com/Anushkasharma101/quotecraft.git" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://github.com/Anushkasharma101/quotecraft.git&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The code is separated into frontend and backend folders so each can be developed and deployed independently.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;How I Built It&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;QuoteCraft uses:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Frontend: HTML, CSS, JavaScript, and Vite.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Backend: Node.js with a JSON API.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Database: MongoDB for products, customers, quotations, and settings.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AI integration: Ollama, configured to use the open-weight qwen2.5:3b model.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The AI integration handles a specific task: interpreting an enquiry and suggesting catalogue product IDs and quantities.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The backend sends the enquiry and catalogue to Ollama and requests a structured JSON response. It then checks the returned product IDs and retrieves prices from the database.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The model interprets the message; application code calculates the money.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Subtotal, tax, and total are calculated by the backend. Users review the proposed items and can correct them before saving.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Saved quotations also contain snapshots of customer details and line items. Changing a catalogue price later does not automatically rewrite an earlier quotation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One important lesson was keeping AI integration separate from deployment. Hosting the web application does not automatically host a local model. That led me to keep demo mode clearly labelled rather than silently falling back when AI is unavailable.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Why Does Open Innovation Matter?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a small business tool, the choice of AI affects both operating costs and how much control the owner has.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Control over inference: With Ollama, the model can run on hardware controlled by the operator. The enquiry does not need to be sent to a closed model API for interpretation. Database privacy still depends on where MongoDB is hosted.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Flexible model choice: The integration is not tied to one model provider. I can experiment with another compatible model and evaluate whether it handles product specifications or customer language better.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A different cost structure: Self-hosting avoids a per-request model API charge. It still has hardware, electricity, or hosting costs, so I wouldn’t describe inference as free.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Room for niche improvements: A quotation assistant needs to understand a business’s catalogue, terminology, and missing requirements. An open approach gives me room to change prompts, validation, and eventually model adaptation around that specific workflow.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I haven’t benchmarked QuoteCraft against a closed API, so I’m not claiming better accuracy or speed. The practical benefit is the ability to build and inspect a quotation workflow around a model I can run and replace.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For me, QuoteCraft is a small, focused experiment: can an open-weight model help a seller spend less time preparing a quote while leaving the business decisions in their hands?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


Beyond Vector Search: Building a Graph-Native Codebase Intelligence Agent with FalkorDB
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Why vector embeddings fail at understanding software architecture, and how we built an explainable ReAct agent that navigates code as a living graph.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


1. Introduction
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every software engineer knows the sinking feeling of opening a new, massive repository for the first time. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You look at dozens of directories, hundreds of source files, implicit architectural layers, and complex dependency webs. You open your favorite AI coding assistant and ask:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;"If I refactor our authentication service, what downstream agent nodes and API routes will break?"&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The assistant pauses, scans your repository with vector similarity search, and responds with a confident, beautifully formatted hallucination. It lists files that don't depend on your service, misses the actual orchestrator three directories over, and leaves you to dig through the call stack manually.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;During the &lt;strong&gt;FalkorDB Graph Hacks 2026&lt;/strong&gt;, we set out to fix this fundamental breakdown. We built &lt;strong&gt;CodeWeaver&lt;/strong&gt; — a graph-powered codebase intelligence agent that treats code not as disconnected text chunks, but as what it actually is: &lt;strong&gt;a directed, typed, multi-dimensional graph&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is the story of why we abandoned vanilla vector RAG for codebase intelligence, how we modeled a real-world enterprise codebase in FalkorDB, and what we learned building an autonomous agent with a 100% explainable reasoning path.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


Demo
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Check out the project demo:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;** Demo Vedio:** &lt;a href="https://youtu.be/0gjnDK7I5U8" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Live Demo&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Demo Preview:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://github.com/NegiSushant/CodeWeaver" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Repo link&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fwydokyyvt35eldmy0wo9.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fwydokyyvt35eldmy0wo9.png" alt=" " width="800" height="339"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


2. The Real Problem: Why Vector Search Fails at Code
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The modern AI stack is obsessed with semantic embeddings. For general knowledge retrieval, vector search works wonders. But software code is not natural language prose — &lt;strong&gt;code is strict, deterministic topology&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When you chunk a codebase into 500-token text slices and embed them into a vector space, you destroy the exact information that makes software function:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Topological Blindness&lt;/strong&gt;: Embedding vectors capture semantic similarity, not causal connectivity. A function named &lt;code&gt;handleAuthError&lt;/code&gt; might be semantically identical to &lt;code&gt;logAuthFailure&lt;/code&gt;, but if only one of them is invoked by the mission-critical state machine, vector search treats them as interchangeable.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The Multi-Hop Horizon&lt;/strong&gt;: Real codebase questions are multi-hop by definition:
$$\text{Repository} \longrightarrow \text{Domain Service} \longrightarrow \text{State Machine Node} \longrightarrow \text{API Route}$$
Vector retrieval cannot jump across four deterministic references. It retrieves chunks that "sound" like the query, completely missing the transitive chain.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Zero Verifiability&lt;/strong&gt;: When a traditional LLM answers an architectural question, you have no way to verify &lt;em&gt;how&lt;/em&gt; it reached its conclusion. You are left trusting a black box with your production deployment.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


3. The Insight: Code is a Graph, Not a Bag of Tokens
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Compilers have understood this for half a century: code represents an Abstract Syntax Tree (AST), a call graph, and an import dependency graph. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When an experienced engineer investigates a codebase, they don't do vector similarity in their heads. They trace references:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;em&gt;"Who calls this function?"&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;em&gt;"Which architectural layer does this file belong to?"&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;em&gt;"What high-level business feature does this module implement?"&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;em&gt;"Who owns this domain?"&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These questions are textbook graph traversals. By representing the codebase as a native property graph, we can leverage Cypher queries to answer structural, multi-hop questions with mathematical certainty. The LLM shouldn't guess connections; it should navigate verified graph relations.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


4. What We Built: CodeWeaver
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;CodeWeaver&lt;/strong&gt; is an autonomous codebase intelligence platform built on top of &lt;strong&gt;FalkorDB&lt;/strong&gt;, Next.js 16, LangChain, and PostgreSQL.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Instead of guessing from isolated snippets, CodeWeaver equips an autonomous ReAct agent with specialized tools that execute Cypher queries against FalkorDB in real time.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Core Capabilities:
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Architectural Discovery&lt;/strong&gt;: Understands clean architecture layers, modules, and file distributions.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Blast Radius &amp;amp; Impact Analysis&lt;/strong&gt;: Calculates multi-hop downstream dependencies when files or functions change.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Feature-to-Code Mapping&lt;/strong&gt;: Bridges the gap between high-level business features (e.g., &lt;em&gt;ReAct Agent Loop&lt;/em&gt;, &lt;em&gt;Human-in-the-Loop Validation&lt;/em&gt;, &lt;em&gt;Policy RAG&lt;/em&gt;) and the exact classes and files that implement them.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Explainable Reasoning Paths&lt;/strong&gt;: Every answer returned by the agent includes the exact internal FalkorDB node IDs traversed. Users can click a single button to inspect the visual subgraph that grounded the response.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Interactive Knowledge Graph Canvas&lt;/strong&gt;: A dedicated, interactive force-directed graph page (&lt;code&gt;/graph&lt;/code&gt;) with category filtering, node inspection, and real-time search.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


5. How We Modeled the Codebase as a Graph
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To test CodeWeaver against a realistic, non-trivial system, we ingested the complete repository of an &lt;strong&gt;Autonomous Healthcare Prior Authorization Agent&lt;/strong&gt; — a system featuring Next.js 16, LangGraph state machines, domain services, and Prisma persistence.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We designed a property graph schema that captures both low-level code syntax and high-level architectural intent:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;(Person)-[:OWNED_BY]-(File|Module)
(File)-[:BELONGS_TO]-&amp;gt;(Module)
(File)-[:IMPLEMENTS]-&amp;gt;(Feature)
(Function|Class)-[:BELONGS_TO]-&amp;gt;(File)
(Function)-[:CALLS]-&amp;gt;(Function)
(Class|File)-[:USES]-&amp;gt;(Class|File)
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h3&gt;


The Node Types:
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;Module&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;: Architectural layers (&lt;code&gt;Agent Core&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;Agent Tools&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;Services&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;Repository&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;UI Components&lt;/code&gt;).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;Feature&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;: Functional product capabilities (&lt;code&gt;ReAct Loop&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;Prior Authorization Flow&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;Clinical Guideline RAG&lt;/code&gt;).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;File&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;: Source files across the repository.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;Function&lt;/code&gt; &amp;amp; &lt;code&gt;Class&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;: Discrete AST-extracted symbols, methods, and declarations.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;Person&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;: Domain owners and maintainers responsible for specific modules.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


The Relationship Topology:
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;BELONGS_TO&lt;/code&gt;: Structural encapsulation (functions in files; files in modules).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;IMPLEMENTS&lt;/code&gt;: Semantic grounding linking low-level code directly to high-level product features.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;CALLS&lt;/code&gt; &amp;amp; &lt;code&gt;USES&lt;/code&gt;: AST invocation chains and import dependencies.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;OWNED_BY&lt;/code&gt;: Accountability and team ownership mapping.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Our ingestion engine parsed the codebase into &lt;strong&gt;228 nodes&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;306 relationships&lt;/strong&gt;, creating a complete structural digital twin inside FalkorDB.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


6. How the Agent Reasons: Cypher-Powered Tools &amp;amp; Explainability
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We built CodeWeaver using LangChain and LangGraph to implement a structured ReAct (Reason-Act-Reflect) loop. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Rather than giving the LLM raw Cypher execution privileges (which often produces invalid syntax and security risks), we provided the agent with four deterministic graph tools:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;getArchitectureOverview&lt;/code&gt;: Traverses high-level &lt;code&gt;Module&lt;/code&gt; nodes and their file counts.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;getFeatureImplementation&lt;/code&gt;: Finds all files and classes linked to a feature via &lt;code&gt;IMPLEMENTS&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;analyzeDependencies&lt;/code&gt;: Executes 1-to-3 hop traversals (&lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;-[:CALLS|USES*1..3]-&lt;/code&gt;) to compute the blast radius of a component.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;getOwnershipInfo&lt;/code&gt;: Resolves code ownership through &lt;code&gt;OWNED_BY&lt;/code&gt; relationships.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;User Prompt
│
▼
LangChain ReAct Agent (Azure OpenAI GPT-4o)
│
├─► Step 1: Select Graph Tool (e.g., analyzeDependencies)
│
├─► Step 2: Query FalkorDB via Redis Protocol
│           MATCH (n)&amp;lt;-[:CALLS|USES*1..2]-(dep)
│           RETURN ID(n), ID(dep), n.name, dep.name
│
├─► Step 3: FalkorDB responds in 1.4ms
│
▼
Synthesize Answer + Collect Reasoning Node IDs: [42, 88, 104, 115]
│
▼
Client: Render Answer + Interactive Reasoning Path Subgraph
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h3&gt;


The Power of the Reasoning Path
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Whenever the agent queries FalkorDB, our Cypher queries explicitly project the internal graph node IDs: &lt;code&gt;RETURN ID(n) AS nodeId&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The agent collects these IDs throughout its reasoning trajectory and returns them alongside the markdown answer. In the UI, the user sees an interactive &lt;strong&gt;Reasoning Path&lt;/strong&gt; badge. Clicking it opens a focused modal containing &lt;em&gt;only&lt;/em&gt; the nodes and relationships the agent touched. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There is zero black-box mystery: developers can verify every claim against the actual graph topology.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


7. Architecture Decisions
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


1. In-Memory Graph Processing with FalkorDB
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Agentic workflows require multiple tool calls per turn. If graph queries take 500ms, an agent that needs 3 hops feels sluggish and unresponsive. FalkorDB runs in-memory with C-level graph algorithms over Redis protocol, consistently returning multi-hop queries in &lt;strong&gt;under 2 milliseconds&lt;/strong&gt;. This sub-millisecond latency makes iterative graph reasoning feel instantaneous.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


2. Separation of Playground (&lt;code&gt;/chat&lt;/code&gt;) and Explorer (&lt;code&gt;/graph&lt;/code&gt;)
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A common mistake in AI developer tools is cramming an enormous canvas into the chat interface. We maintained a strict separation:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The &lt;strong&gt;Playground&lt;/strong&gt; focuses on focused conversational Q&amp;amp;A with lightweight, contextual subgraph previews.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The &lt;strong&gt;Knowledge Graph Page&lt;/strong&gt; provides an expansive, full-viewport canvas with category filtering, real-time node search, and full property inspection.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


3. Persistent Grok-Style Chat Sessions
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Codebase investigations are iterative. We paired PostgreSQL and Prisma with our FalkorDB backend to persist chat sessions, conversation histories, and associated reasoning node IDs across browser sessions.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


8. Challenges We Faced &amp;amp; How We Solved Them
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Challenge 1: The "Visual Hairball" Effect
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Our first attempt at visualizing 200+ nodes and 300+ relationships on an HTML canvas resulted in an unreadable green blob. Text labels overlapped, and users couldn't isolate relevant components.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Solution&lt;/strong&gt;: We introduced adaptive scale-based label rendering and category filtering. In &lt;code&gt;GraphViewer.tsx&lt;/code&gt;, high-level entities (&lt;code&gt;Module&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;Feature&lt;/code&gt;) display bold labels at all zoom levels, while granular labels (&lt;code&gt;Function&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;Class&lt;/code&gt;) only render when the user zooms in past &lt;code&gt;1.05x&lt;/code&gt; scale. We also added canvas outline stroke rendering (&lt;code&gt;ctx.strokeText&lt;/code&gt;) to ensure contrast across both light and dark themes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Challenge 2: Hallucination-Free Reasoning Nodes
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Initially, we experimented with asking the LLM to output which nodes it referenced. The result? The LLM occasionally invented node IDs that didn't exist in FalkorDB.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Solution&lt;/strong&gt;: We eliminated LLM discretion entirely. The graph tools themselves extract and accumulate the exact FalkorDB &lt;code&gt;ID(node)&lt;/code&gt; integers directly from the Cypher query result records. The frontend receives mathematically grounded node IDs straight from the database engine.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


9. What We Learned
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Graph databases turn RAG from probabilistic guessing into deterministic retrieval.&lt;/strong&gt; When an LLM cites a structural path derived from a graph database, its hallucinations on architectural boundaries drop to near zero.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Explainability is the missing pillar in developer tooling.&lt;/strong&gt; Developers don't distrust AI because it's imperfect; they distrust AI because it doesn't show its work. Providing an interactive subgraph showing the exact nodes used in reasoning builds immediate trust.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Graph schema design requires intentional abstraction.&lt;/strong&gt; Including every single variable or line of code creates unusable noise. Focusing on high-impact abstractions (&lt;code&gt;Module&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;Feature&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;File&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;Function&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;Class&lt;/code&gt;) provides the sweet spot for agentic reasoning.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


10. Why FalkorDB Was Critical
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;CodeWeaver simply could not exist without a purpose-built graph database:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Expressive OpenCypher&lt;/strong&gt;: Modeling queries like &lt;code&gt;MATCH (f:File)-[:IMPLEMENTS]-&amp;gt;(feat:Feature {name: "ReAct Agent Loop"})&lt;/code&gt; requires clean, standardized graph query language semantics.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Unbeatable Speed&lt;/strong&gt;: FalkorDB’s in-memory execution meant our agent’s multi-step ReAct loop ran without latency penalties.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Effortless Docker Deployment&lt;/strong&gt;: Deploying FalkorDB alongside Postgres in a single &lt;code&gt;docker compose up -d&lt;/code&gt; made local setup seamless.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;FalkorDB isn't a secondary cache in CodeWeaver — it is the intelligence engine that powers every single answer our agent produces.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


11. Conclusion
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Software systems will only grow larger, more complex, and more distributed. As AI agents take on larger roles in software engineering, feeding them flattened text chunks through vector search is a dead end.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;By anchoring AI reasoning in a native graph database like &lt;strong&gt;FalkorDB&lt;/strong&gt;, we can build agents that truly understand software architecture — agents that trace dependencies across multiple hops, respect module boundaries, and show their work with complete transparency.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;CodeWeaver proves that when code is modeled as connected knowledge, AI stops guessing and starts understanding.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Check out the &lt;a href="https://github.com/NegiSushant/CodeWeaver" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;CodeWeaver GitHub Repository&lt;/a&gt; to explore the code, run the ingestion pipeline, and try the graph-powered agent yourself.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A CRM integration is useful when the next team receives a usable record and can recover when something goes wrong. An HTTP success response alone does not establish that outcome.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This checklist from &lt;strong&gt;JC TechLabs&lt;/strong&gt; is for developers and operations teams planning website-to-CRM, CRM-to-ERP or campaign-to-sales workflows.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


1. Define the business event
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Write an acceptance statement before choosing a connector. For example: a valid enquiry creates or updates one lead, preserves the permitted campaign context, assigns the correct team and records the hand-off status. Agree who owns the workflow and what the operating team considers a successful hand-off.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


2. Map fields and ownership
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For each field, record its source, destination, type, required status, transformation, validation and owner. Decide whether an empty value means leave unchanged or clear this field.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A CRM might own the sales stage while accounting owns invoice status. Two-way synchronization needs explicit conflict rules and protection against update loops. Time zones, currencies, phone numbers and non-English text need deliberate treatment.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We published an &lt;a href="https://jctechlabs.com/assets/integration-field-map.csv" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;editable field-mapping CSV&lt;/a&gt; with fictional example rows. It contains no customer data.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


3. Separate event identity from customer identity
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A timeout can leave the sender unsure whether the receiver created a record. Give the event a stable identifier and use a destination-supported idempotency mechanism or a deduplication layer appropriate to the workflow.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An event identifier prevents replaying the same operation. A customer-matching policy determines whether two enquiries belong to the same person or organisation. Those are separate problems. Shared email addresses and changed contact details make automatic merging risky.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://docs.stripe.com/api/idempotent_requests" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Stripe’s idempotent request documentation&lt;/a&gt; is a concrete provider example. Check the actual platform’s retention, retry and error behavior rather than assuming every API works the same way.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


4. Make failures recoverable
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Distinguish temporary failures from invalid requests. An outage or rate limit may justify bounded retries with backoff and provider guidance. A revoked credential, invalid value or missing permission needs correction.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A failed-job queue should expose the event identifier, processing state, attempt count and a safe diagnostic reason. Provide an authorised way to correct and replay an event. Avoid logging secrets or unnecessary customer content.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Reconciliation is also necessary: compare accepted source events with destination records over an agreed period, including delayed events and duplicates. Alerts alone can miss a silently lost hand-off.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


5. Limit access and transferred data
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Use a dedicated integration identity where supported and grant only the required permissions. Agree credential storage, rotation, revocation and vendor handover.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Transfer the fields the receiving application needs. Define deletion behavior and access to customer records. If an AI step summarises an enquiry, specify which provider receives the text and how its output is reviewed before it changes a business record.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;a href="https://api-security.owasp.org/editions/2023/en/0x11-t10/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;OWASP API Security Top 10&lt;/a&gt; provides a useful risk framework, including object-level permissions and unsafe treatment of third-party API responses.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


6. Keep attribution and consent separate
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Record the original acquisition source separately from later interactions. Preserve an explicit unknown state when attribution is unavailable. A campaign source does not establish marketing permission. Carry consent scope and capture time where required, respect withdrawals downstream and keep personal customer information out of campaign URLs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


7. Test the awkward cases
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Include repeated delivery, invalid fields, destination outages, rate limits, expired credentials, delayed events and conflicting edits. Use synthetic records and verify the actual destination record, ownership and recovery state.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For launch, agree the backfill, cutover, reconciliation and rollback conditions. Turning off a connector may not undo records it already created.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


8. Hand over something the team can operate
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Deliver the field map, ownership rules, integration diagram, retry behavior, access inventory, test evidence and recovery instructions. Name the owners of connected systems and agree how API or field changes are communicated.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Include ongoing connector subscriptions, API usage, monitoring and maintenance in the cost discussion. Review failed events, duplicates and hand-off times after launch.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;a href="https://jctechlabs.com/insights/crm-integration-checklist/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;full CRM integration guide&lt;/a&gt; includes the checklist and related resources. Our &lt;a href="https://jctechlabs.com/tools/automation-planner/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;automation capacity planner&lt;/a&gt; makes workload, human-review and operating-cost assumptions explicit before a pilot. Its figures are illustrative scenarios, not client outcomes or promised savings.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Published by the official JC TechLabs account. Prepared with AI assistance and checked against the linked technical references. Examples are illustrative.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This is a submission for the &lt;a href="https://dev.to/challenges/hacktoberfest-week2-2026-10-12"&gt;Hacktoberfest Open-Source AI Challenge Week 2: Side Hustle&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What I Built
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Listing Writer&lt;/strong&gt; is a local-first, AI-powered listing generator for Etsy and eBay sellers. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Imagine a vintage reseller or a handmade crafter with 20 items to photograph and list over the weekend. They don't have a copywriter. They use a generic cloud AI tool, but it forces them to upload photos of unreleased inventory to a server they don't control. Worse, generic AI often hallucinates details—confidently writing "approximately 8 inches wide" for an item it cannot actually measure.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Listing Writer solves this by running entirely on the seller's laptop. It uses an open-weight vision model to read the photo, generate a title, description, tags, and price range, and streams the result instantly. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But its most important feature is &lt;strong&gt;honesty&lt;/strong&gt;: if the model cannot see the size, material, or quantity in the photo, it writes &lt;code&gt;[seller to confirm]&lt;/code&gt; instead of guessing. The seller gets a perfect starting point without being fed AI hallucinations.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Demo
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;(Note: Because I am running Gemma 3 locally on my CPU, text generation takes about 90 seconds. The video is trimmed to show the setup, the offline proof, and the final result.)&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gXQ9E0Teu8s" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gXQ9E0Teu8s&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Code
&lt;/h2&gt;


&lt;div class="crayons-card c-embed text-styles text-styles--secondary"&gt;
&lt;div class="c-embed__content"&gt;
&lt;div class="c-embed__cover"&gt;
