&lt;p&gt;How we handled generating 100+ styled QR codes and packaging them into a ZIP entirely in the browser.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Have you ever needed to generate 50 QR codes for event badges, or 100 codes for a marketing campaign? &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Most tools require you to create an account, upload your data to their server, and generate them one by one. That's slow, frustrating, and a privacy nightmare.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We wanted to build something better: a &lt;strong&gt;100% client-side Batch QR Generator&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here's a look at what we built and how it handles scale without touching a backend.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


The Workflow
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Input:&lt;/strong&gt; Paste a list of URLs, upload a CSV, or use manual entry.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Style:&lt;/strong&gt; Apply a unified design (colors, eye markers, logo) to &lt;em&gt;every&lt;/em&gt; QR code in the batch simultaneously.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Generate:&lt;/strong&gt; Click one button to render up to 100 unique styled QR codes.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Export:&lt;/strong&gt; Bundle all the QR codes into a single &lt;code&gt;.zip&lt;/code&gt; file or download a print-ready PDF.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


The Technical Challenge: Client-Side Scaling
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Generating one QR code in the browser is easy. Generating 100+ high-resolution styled QR codes, rendering them to canvas, and packaging them into a ZIP file without freezing the UI requires a bit of care.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We utilized:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;qr-code-styling&lt;/code&gt; for generating the vector and raster outputs.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;jszip&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;file-saver&lt;/code&gt; to package the outputs directly in memory.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Asynchronous processing to ensure the browser UI doesn't lock up while processing the batch.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No servers. No data limits. Just pure client-side speed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Why Client-Side Matters
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When you're generating QR codes for a business, your data (URLs, text payloads, UTM parameters) is often sensitive. By keeping the entire process in the browser, your data never leaves your machine.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Try It Out
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You can test the tool right now. It's free forever, requires no login, and has no ads.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;👉 &lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://rahalune-qr-studio.vercel.app/batch" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Try the RahaLune Batch QR Generator&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'd love to hear your feedback on the implementation or any features you'd like to see added next!&lt;br&gt;
&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fbvtcgpr5iguidrnhxhji.jpg" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fbvtcgpr5iguidrnhxhji.jpg" alt=" " width="800" height="447"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I run &lt;a href="https://clockaura.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;ClockAura&lt;/a&gt;, a free site of online timers: countdowns, a Pomodoro timer, a stopwatch, interval timers for the gym. The first versions had the bug I suspect every browser timer has at some point: start a countdown, switch to another tab, and it rings late. Sometimes minutes late.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The bug wasn't in one line of code. It came from an assumption: that the browser keeps running your code at the same pace when nobody is looking. It doesn't. This post covers what I changed and why. The code snippets are lightly trimmed from the real hook that runs every countdown on the site.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The naive version
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My first countdown looked like most tutorials:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Don't do this&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setInterval&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;setRemaining&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1000&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1000&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Two separate things are wrong here.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;First, &lt;code&gt;setInterval(fn, 1000)&lt;/code&gt; doesn't promise a call every 1000 ms. It promises &lt;em&gt;at least&lt;/em&gt; 1000 ms between calls. Every late call adds a little drift, and subtracting a fixed 1000 bakes that drift into the result. Over a 25-minute Pomodoro you can lose whole seconds even in a foreground tab.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Second, background tabs. Chrome limits timers in hidden tabs to roughly one wake-up per second, and after a page has been hidden for a few minutes it can batch chained timers much more aggressively, down to about once a minute. Firefox and Safari have their own versions of this. A countdown that counts &lt;em&gt;ticks&lt;/em&gt; is at the mercy of how many ticks the browser lets you have.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Rule 1: store the end, not the remaining
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The fix most people land on, and the one that matters most: when the timer starts, compute the moment it should end, and from then on derive everything from that moment.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;running&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;targetRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;performance&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;now&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;remaining&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;liveRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;rafRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;requestAnimationFrame&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;tick&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;armBackup&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// cleanup cancels the frame and the timeout&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;running&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;tick&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Each tick then asks "how far are we from the end?" instead of "subtract one more second":&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;tick&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useCallback&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;liveRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;left&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Math&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;max&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;targetRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;performance&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;now&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;());&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;setRemaining&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;left&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;left&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="cm"&gt;/* finish: see Rule 3 */&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;rafRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;requestAnimationFrame&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;tick&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[]);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;A late tick no longer causes drift. If a frame arrives 300 ms late, the display jumps 300 ms and is correct again. Nothing accumulates.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I use &lt;code&gt;performance.now()&lt;/code&gt; here, not &lt;code&gt;Date.now()&lt;/code&gt;. &lt;code&gt;performance.now()&lt;/code&gt; is monotonic: it doesn't move when the user's clock syncs with a time server or when someone changes the system time. &lt;code&gt;Date.now()&lt;/code&gt; can jump backwards or forwards, and a countdown that gains five minutes because Windows corrected the clock is a fun bug report to read.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There is one place where I do use &lt;code&gt;Date.now()&lt;/code&gt;: when a running timer is saved to &lt;code&gt;localStorage&lt;/code&gt; so it can be resumed after a reload. &lt;code&gt;performance.now()&lt;/code&gt; restarts at zero on every page load, so it can't be stored. The saved record keeps a wall-clock &lt;code&gt;endsAt&lt;/code&gt;, and the page turns it back into a duration when you come back:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;left&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;t&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;endsAt&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;t&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;endsAt&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;now&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;t&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;left&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


Rule 2: don't trust requestAnimationFrame to finish the job
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I moved the display loop to &lt;code&gt;requestAnimationFrame&lt;/code&gt; because it lines up with the screen refresh and makes the digits look smooth. The catch: browsers pause rAF entirely in hidden tabs. Not slow it down. Pause it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;With a rAF-only loop, the end-timestamp math is still correct, but nothing runs the check. So the timer "finishes" the moment you switch back to the tab, which might be ten minutes after it should have rung. That was most of the late-alarm reports.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The fix is a second, much simpler mechanism: one &lt;code&gt;setTimeout&lt;/code&gt; aimed straight at the end time.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;armBackup&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useCallback&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;clearTimeout&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;backupRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;liveRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;left&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;targetRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;performance&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;now&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;backupRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setTimeout&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;liveRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;targetRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;performance&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;now&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;tick&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;else&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;armBackup&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Math&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;max&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;left&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;15&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;tick&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;A few details that took me more than one try:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;One long timeout, not a ticking one.&lt;/strong&gt; Background throttling hits chained timers hardest: timers that schedule timers, again and again. A single timeout set for 25 minutes is about the friendliest thing you can ask a throttled tab for. At worst it gets lined up with the next one-second boundary, which is fine for a kitchen timer and a Pomodoro.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The +15 ms.&lt;/strong&gt; Timer callbacks and &lt;code&gt;performance.now()&lt;/code&gt; don't share an exact clock, and &lt;code&gt;performance.now()&lt;/code&gt; is deliberately coarsened for security reasons. Without the padding, the timeout sometimes fired a hair &lt;em&gt;before&lt;/em&gt; the target, saw 2 ms left, and had to re-arm. The padding makes the common case a single wake-up.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Re-arm if early.&lt;/strong&gt; If it does fire early anyway, it schedules itself again for the real remainder instead of giving up or ringing early.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When the user adds a minute with the +1 button, the end moves, so the backup has to move too:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;addMs&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useCallback&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;deltaMs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;setRemaining&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;prev&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;next&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Math&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;max&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;prev&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;deltaMs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;targetRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;next&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;prev&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;next&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;setTimeout&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;armBackup&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// end time moved: re-aim the backstop&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;armBackup&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The &lt;code&gt;setTimeout(armBackup, 0)&lt;/code&gt; is there because the state updater runs later than you might expect. Re-arming on the next turn means the backup reads the updated target.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Rule 3: two ways to finish means two ways to ring twice
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Once both rAF and &lt;code&gt;setTimeout&lt;/code&gt; can end the timer, you have a race. Come back to the tab at exactly the wrong moment and both fire within a few milliseconds. The user hears the alarm twice and the "focus minutes" stat would count the session twice.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The guard is one ref that both paths check, and the finishing path flips it &lt;em&gt;first&lt;/em&gt;, before doing anything else:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;left&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;liveRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;          &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// close the door first&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;clearTimeout&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;backupRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;  &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// cancel the other path&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;cancelAnimationFrame&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;rafRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;setRunning&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;onDoneRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;              &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// ring exactly once&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;It has to be a ref, not state. State updates are batched and applied on the next render, so two callbacks in the same turn would both read the old &lt;code&gt;running === true&lt;/code&gt;. A ref is just a mutable box, and the second caller sees &lt;code&gt;false&lt;/code&gt; immediately.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;onDone&lt;/code&gt; lives in a ref for a related reason: the page passes a fresh callback on every render, and I didn't want &lt;code&gt;tick&lt;/code&gt; to be recreated 60 times a second because of it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Rule 4: keep the screen on, but only when it matters
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A lot of timers end up on a projector or a TV: &lt;a href="https://clockaura.com/classroom-mode" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;classroom timers&lt;/a&gt;, workout timers across the room. Nothing kills that faster than the laptop dimming its screen at minute nine.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Screen Wake Lock API fixes it in a few lines, with two catches. The lock is released automatically whenever the page is hidden, and it's not re-acquired when you come back. So the hook listens for &lt;code&gt;visibilitychange&lt;/code&gt; and asks again:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;onVisible&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;active&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;document&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;visibilityState&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;visible&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;lockRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;acquire&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;document&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;addEventListener&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;visibilitychange&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;onVisible&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The other catch is about scope. The lock is held while a timer runs &lt;em&gt;or&lt;/em&gt; while the page is full screen, and released otherwise. A wake lock that stays on after the timer ends drains a laptop battery for nothing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Rule 5: the service worker must never serve an old timer
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Timers are the kind of page people open with bad Wi-Fi: in a gym basement, a classroom, a kitchen. So the site has a service worker that caches the core timer pages after the first visit, and they open offline.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The thing I was most nervous about was the opposite problem: shipping a fix and having half my users stuck on the cached old page. The rules I settled on:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;HTML is network-first.&lt;/strong&gt; The cached copy is only used if the network fails, or on a repeat visit when the network hasn't answered within 700 ms.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Hashed assets are cache-first.&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;code&gt;/assets/index-a1b2c3d4.js&lt;/code&gt; never changes content, so caching it forever is safe.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The cache name includes the build id.&lt;/strong&gt; Every deploy creates a new cache and the &lt;code&gt;activate&lt;/code&gt; handler deletes the others, so a cached page always matches the scripts cached with it.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;VERSION&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;__BUILD_ID__&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;           &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// replaced at build time&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;CACHE&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;`clockaura-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;VERSION&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nb"&gt;self&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;addEventListener&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;activate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;event&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;event&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;waitUntil&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;async &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;keys&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;caches&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;keys&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;all&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;keys&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;filter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;k&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;k&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;CACHE&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;map&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;k&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;caches&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;delete&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;k&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)));&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;self&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;clients&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;claim&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;})());&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The service worker has nothing to do with timing accuracy, but it's part of the same promise: when you press Start, the timer works, whatever the tab or the network is doing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


One gap I found while writing this
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The tab title ("04:59 · Countdown") and the little progress ring in the favicon were updated from the same render loop as the digits, and that loop is driven by rAF. In a background tab the &lt;em&gt;ending&lt;/em&gt; was reliable, thanks to the backup timeout, but the title could sit still until you looked at the tab again. Ironically, a background tab is exactly when you'd look at the title. The fix is a one-second interval that only runs while &lt;code&gt;document.hidden&lt;/code&gt; is true and only updates the title. Background throttling allows one wake-up per second, and that's all a title needs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The short version
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you're building anything that counts down in a browser:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Store the end time and derive the remaining time from it. Never subtract fixed ticks.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Use &lt;code&gt;performance.now()&lt;/code&gt; inside a page and &lt;code&gt;Date.now()&lt;/code&gt; only for anything you save.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Drive the display with rAF, but back up the finish with one &lt;code&gt;setTimeout&lt;/code&gt; aimed at the end.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Guard the finish with a ref so it can only happen once.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Re-acquire the wake lock on &lt;code&gt;visibilitychange&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you want to see it all working, start a &lt;a href="https://clockaura.com/pomodoro-timer" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Pomodoro timer&lt;/a&gt;, switch to another tab, and wait for the bell. Or open a link timer like &lt;a href="https://clockaura.com/t/25m" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;clockaura.com/t/25m&lt;/a&gt;, which starts a 25-minute countdown straight from the URL.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'm happy to answer questions in the comments, especially if you've found a browser where this still rings late.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Building a modern website with &lt;a href="https://react.dev/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;React&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="https://nextjs.org/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Next.js&lt;/a&gt;, and &lt;a href="https://ui.shadcn.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;shadcn/ui&lt;/a&gt; often means spending time assembling components, adjusting layouts, and repeating the same design work across pages.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What if you could put your pages together visually, customize the content directly in a live preview, and export the code when you are ready?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That is what &lt;a href="https://shadcnspace.com/builder" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Shadcn Builder&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt; from &lt;a class="mentioned-user" href="https://dev.to/suniljoshi19"&gt;@suniljoshi19&lt;/a&gt; is designed to help you do.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is a drag-and-drop page builder that lets you create web pages using ready-to-use UI blocks and components. You can arrange sections, edit content, preview your design, and export code for your React or Next.js project without building every section from scratch.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What is Shadcn Builder?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is a visual page-building tool powered by shadcnspace team. It helps developers and designers assemble complete web pages using customizable blocks instead of creating every layout manually.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Whether you are working on a SaaS landing page, a startup website, or a marketing page, you can start with existing UI sections and adapt them to your project.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;With Shadcn Builder, you can:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;Drag and drop ready-to-use UI blocks and components.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;Create single landing pages or multi-page websites.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;Edit headings, text, images in the preview.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;Preview your layout before exporting.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;Export reusable code for your React or Next.js project.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The goal is simple: spend less time assembling layouts and more time building your product.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How to Use Shadcn Builder
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You can create and export your pages in three simple steps.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;  &lt;iframe src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/5OBJJnN3Tpo" width="710" height="399"&gt;
&lt;/iframe&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Step 1: Build Your Page with UI Blocks
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Start by opening - &lt;a href="https://shadcnspace.com/builder" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://shadcnspace.com/builder&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Choose the UI blocks and components that fit your project, then drag and drop them into your page layout. You can combine different sections to create a complete page without starting from an empty canvas.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Ffp89v57v8hbpqxp3qib3.webp" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Ffp89v57v8hbpqxp3qib3.webp" alt="Build Your Page with shadcn builder" width="736" height="298"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, you can assemble a landing page using a hero section, feature sections, testimonials, pricing, and a footer.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Instead of coding each section individually, you can reuse existing blocks and focus on creating a layout that fits your needs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Step 2: Customize Your Layout Visually
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Once you have added your blocks, customize the page to match your design.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You can edit headings, update text, change images, and refine your content directly in the preview. The interactive preview helps you see how your changes affect the page as you work.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fxn1f1hcuh9aw3fdkfqq8.webp" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fxn1f1hcuh9aw3fdkfqq8.webp" alt="edit section with shadcn builder" width="736" height="298"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You can also build multiple connected pages while keeping your website's design consistent.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F688ul3y50uxzc3ke0lbe.webp" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F688ul3y50uxzc3ke0lbe.webp" alt="edit text with shadcn builder" width="736" height="298"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This makes it easier to experiment with different layouts and content before exporting the code.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Step 3: Preview and Export Your Code
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When your page looks right, preview the result and export the code.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Shadcn Builder provides clean, reusable code that you can bring into your React or Next.js project and continue customizing to fit your application.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fyywe9p9hb3v6vah7t3kr.webp" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fyywe9p9hb3v6vah7t3kr.webp" alt="Preview and Export Your Code shadcn builder" width="736" height="298"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You can keep working with the exported code in your own development environment, giving you control over how your project evolves.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No need to treat your visual design as a finished, locked-in product. Use the builder to speed up the initial layout work, then continue developing your application the way you prefer.&lt;/p&gt;

